{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794348783071,"sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","part":{"id":"prt_1c78595c6001Ice83je2qty7jw","messageID":"msg_1c785826f001ZVSgJnhpdQa5LJ","sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794348783071,"sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","part":{"id":"prt_1c78595c9001YwDKtpWKHvjUN0","messageID":"msg_1c785826f001ZVSgJnhpdQa5LJ","sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","type":"text","text":"<think>The kill worked (SIGTERM to the old server), but the new server didn't start — probably because the `& disown` + `setsid` combination with the `kill` in the same command line... Actually the issue: the command `kill $(pgrep -f \"gunicorn.*app:app\")` — pgrep matched the bash command itself? The output shows the server was terminated. Then the new server should have started... but there's no new log entry after 22:11:55. Wait, the log shows the shutdown at 22:11:55 — that was the OLD server (started 22:11:36) receiving SIGTERM. The new server's output would overwrite the log (`>` truncates). Since the log still shows the old server's content, the new server never started or its redirect didn't truncate... Hmm, actually the new server would truncate the file at start. The log still contains old content → the new server didn't start.\n\nMaybe the whole command was killed when the tool timed out at 60s? The order: `rm -rf data; kill ...; sleep 1; start server & disown; sleep 3; run smoke test`. The smoke test may have hung (e.g., waiting on curl) — no output because the tail buffered? Then the tool killed the command group at 60s, killing the new server too.\n\nWhy would the smoke test hang? Possibly `curl` to the health endpoint when the server was down... no, curl would fail fast. Hmm, `curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\"` — if the connection is refused, curl exits quickly.\n\nWait — maybe the issue is that the smoke test's `set -euo pipefail` plus a failing command aborts. But it printed nothing at all, which suggests it never ran (the server start blocked?). `setsid nohup python3 -m gunicorn ... &` — setsid detaches; that worked before.\n\nHmm, maybe the issue is that the tool's shell waits for all background processes to finish before returning, and the earlier successful run had the same structure and returned quickly... The difference: this time I had `kill $(pgrep ...)` which may have matched the shell's own process (pgrep -f \"gunicorn.*app:app\" matches the bash -c command line containing that string!). Yes! The bash command line itself contains \"gunicorn.*app:app\" as part of the pgrep pattern → pgrep matched the bash process → `kill` killed my own shell! That's why the command died with no output.\n\nSo: avoid pgrep patterns matching my own command line. Use a different approach: `pkill -f \"python3 -m gunicorn\"` also matches. I'll store the PID in a file instead.\n\nLet me restart the server with a PID file.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794348783049,"end":1794348783067}}}
