{"type":"text","timestamp":1794348962600,"sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","part":{"id":"prt_1c7885316001nvDqhVqcldmcQ0","messageID":"msg_1c7884085001i0aZQ5sZn0bCxF","sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","type":"text","text":"<think>Now let me set up a quick DOM-level test to catch runtime errors. Options:\n1. Install jsdom temporarily and write a small test script that renders the App with a mocked fetch.\n2. Use `react-dom/server` renderToString to check the initial render doesn't throw (only tests the signed-out state since localStorage isn't available in SSR).\n\nOption 1 with jsdom gives real coverage of the signed-in flow. Let me install jsdom as a temp dev dependency, write a test script under /workspace/frontend/scripts/smoke.mjs, run it, then remove jsdom from package.json and re-run npm install to clean the lock.\n\nActually — is it worth it? The risk of a runtime error in the signed-in path is real (e.g., `session.user.is_admin` when user is undefined). Let me do a quick jsdom test; it's valuable.\n\nPlan for the test script:\n- Set up jsdom global environment (window, document, navigator, localStorage).\n- Stub global.fetch to talk to the real backend at http://127.0.0.1:8000 (the backend is running). But the app calls `/api/...` relative URLs; in jsdom, fetch relative URLs fail. I can stub fetch to prefix the base URL and delegate to node's fetch (Node 18+ has global fetch, and undici supports relative URLs? No). I'll write a stub: `global.fetch = (url, init) => realFetch('http://127.0.0.1:8000' + url, init)`.\n- Render App with react-dom/client into a jsdom container.\n- Wait for effects, then assert the DOM shows the auth panel; fill in the form and submit; assert the header appears; etc.\n\nWriting this with plain jsdom + React requires care with act() and timers. It's doable but fiddly. React 19's `act` is available from \"react\".\n\nLet me do it — install jsdom temporarily.\n\nActually, maybe simpler and quite effective: use the `react-dom/server` renderToString for static checks + a jsdom-based interactive test. Let me just do the jsdom test with act.\n\nLet me install jsdom.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794348962582,"end":1794348962596}}}
