{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794348991621,"sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","part":{"id":"prt_1c788c4720011esoHqdq0myUnX","messageID":"msg_1c788a959001V0rbwgf4xuUMOL","sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794348991621,"sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","part":{"id":"prt_1c788c474001Y1SIPENZ39yZDK","messageID":"msg_1c788a959001V0rbwgf4xuUMOL","sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","type":"text","text":"<think>Problem: importing \"../src/App.tsx\" from a .mjs file won't work — Node can't parse TSX. Options:\n- Use vite to build a test bundle... complex.\n- Use esbuild (bundled with vite) to transform the TSX on the fly. Vite ships esbuild as a dependency. I can use `esbuild` from node_modules to build the App into a temp ESM file, then import it.\n\nLet me check if esbuild is available in node_modules (vite depends on esbuild). Yes, vite 8 uses rolldown? Vite 8 might use rolldown instead of esbuild... Vite 8 (rolldown-vite?) — the package is \"vite\": \"8.3.5\". Vite 7+ still uses esbuild for transforms; Vite 8 may use rolldown/oxc. Let me check node_modules for esbuild or rolldown.\n\nSimpler approach: use Vite's own build API to build the app for the test, or use `vite-node`? Not installed.\n\nAlternative simplest approach: build the frontend with vite (already works) and then run the DOM test against the built bundle (dist/assets/index-*.js) loaded into jsdom with `runScripts: \"dangerously\"` and a stubbed fetch. The built bundle is an ES module; jsdom doesn't support ES modules in scripts. I could build with `vite build --format iife`? Not directly supported for app builds... Actually Vite supports `build.rollupOptions.output.format = 'iife'` for library mode only. Hmm, for app builds with a single entry and no code splitting, `format: 'iife'` works if there's no module preload. Let me try: vite build with rollupOptions.output.format = 'iife' — for an app build, Rollup can do IIFE if there's a single chunk and no dynamic imports. CSS would be extracted separately (fine).\n\nThat gives a classic script that jsdom can execute with runScripts: 'dangerously'. The bundle uses fetch → I need to stub window.fetch before the script runs (jsdom's window has no fetch by default; I can define it).\n\nThis approach tests the real production bundle. Let me do that.\n\nSteps:\n1. Create a temp vite config for the iife build: `vite build --config vite.test.config.ts` outputting to /workspace/frontend/dist-test.\n2. In the test script: create a JSDOM with runScripts: 'dangerously', resources: 'usable'? The script is external (src=/assets/index.js) → jsdom can load it if resources: 'usable' and the URL is a file:// or served. Simpler: read the built JS file and inject it as an inline <script> in the HTML. Inline script with runScripts: 'dangerously' executes. But the built code may reference `document.currentScript` for modulepreload polyfill — fine.\n\nCareful: the built bundle might use `import.meta` — in IIFE format that's not allowed; Vite would error or warn. Let me try.\n\nLet me check if esbuild is available first — that'd be simpler (transform TSX → JS, then import in Node with jsdom globals).</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794348991604,"end":1794348991618}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794348991648,"sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01a1c788c257727ea86f32a4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls node_modules | grep -i -E \"esbuild|rolldown|oxc\" ; ls node_modules/.bin | head -20","workdir":"/workspace/frontend"},"output":"@oxc-project\n@rolldown\nrolldown\njiti\nnanoid\nrolldown\ntldts\nvite\n","metadata":{"output":"@oxc-project\n@rolldown\nrolldown\njiti\nnanoid\nrolldown\ntldts\nvite\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls node_modules | grep -i -E \"esbuild|rolldown|oxc\" ; ls node_modules/.bin | head -20","time":{"start":1794348991612,"end":1794348991630}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c788c477001JbmOXRYyEDloq5","sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","messageID":"msg_1c788a959001V0rbwgf4xuUMOL"}}
