{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794349016418,"sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","part":{"id":"prt_1c7892552001rTXZGOdj2M6R3F","messageID":"msg_1c788c49c0017MjxFG8yYCWA3u","sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794349016418,"sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","part":{"id":"prt_1c7892554001gYk3yrvM0kUKA6","messageID":"msg_1c788c49c0017MjxFG8yYCWA3u","sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","type":"text","text":"<think>No esbuild, but rolldown is available (Vite 8 uses rolldown). I can use rolldown's Node API to bundle the TSX for the test, or simply use vite's `build` API programmatically with `build.lib` format iife.\n\nLet me use Vite's JS API in the test script:\n\n```js\nimport { build } from \"vite\";\nawait build({\n  root: \"frontend\",\n  configFile: false,\n  plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],\n  build: {\n    lib: { entry: \"src/App.tsx\", formats: [\"iife\"], name: \"App\", fileName: () => \"app.js\" },\n    outDir: \"dist-test\",\n    emptyOutDir: true,\n    cssCodeSplit: false,\n  },\n});\n```\nWith lib mode, the entry exports the default export as `window.App`. Then in jsdom, inject the script and call `window.App()` to render with React from... hmm, the lib bundle includes React itself (since it's not external) → React would be bundled inside → the bundle's React instance differs from the Node-side React I import for createRoot. I'd have to render using the bundle's own React. In lib mode with `name: \"App\"`, the bundle exposes the module's exports. I could instead make the test entry a small file that imports React, react-dom/client, and App and renders it:\n\ntest entry: `src/test-entry.tsx`:\n```tsx\nimport { createRoot } from \"react-dom/client\";\nimport App from \"./App\";\nexport function mount(el: HTMLElement) { createRoot(el).render(<App />); }\n```\nThen lib mode exposes window.TestEntry.mount(el).\n\nThat's clean. Let me create that temp entry (not shipped — put it in scripts/ so it isn't part of the app build; but the lib build would include it; fine, I'll delete it afterwards... or keep it in scripts/ — harmless, but it would be included in the repo. I'll delete it after testing).\n\nLet me write:\n- /workspace/frontend/scripts/test-entry.tsx (temporary)\n- /workspace/frontend/scripts/dom_smoke.mjs (builds via the Vite API then runs jsdom)\n\nIn jsdom, after injecting the IIFE bundle, I call `window.TestEntry.mount(document.getElementById('root'))`, then wait for React to render. Since React's rendering is async (concurrent), I need to flush: awaiting a macrotask (setTimeout) should let React commit. React 18+ concurrent rendering schedules work via MessageChannel/scheduler → in jsdom, MessageChannel exists → the scheduler will run tasks. Awaiting several setTimeout(0) cycles should be enough.\n\n`act()` isn't available from the bundled React unless exposed. I can expose it: in the test entry, export `act` too... `act` from \"react\" requires IS_REACT_ACT_ENVIRONMENT = true to avoid warnings; it works in production builds? `react.act` exists in the react package (not just the dev build). But the production build of React still supports act? The `act` function is available in react's production bundle? In React 19, `React.act` exists but warns if IS_REACT_ACT_ENVIRONMENT isn't set... Let me not use act; just flush with timers.\n\nAlternative: skip act entirely and use `await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 50))` after each interaction, then check the DOM. React's event handlers (click dispatch) are processed synchronously in the DOM event → state updates → React re-renders (concurrent → scheduled). The scheduler uses MessageChannel in the browser env → jsdom supports MessageChannel → tasks run on the macrotask queue. So awaiting setTimeout should flush renders.\n\nLet's do it. Also set `globalThis.IS_REACT_ACT_ENVIRONMENT`? Not needed without act.\n\nFor the fetch stub: jsdom's window doesn't have fetch (jsdom doesn't implement fetch). I'll define window.fetch = (url, init) => nodeFetch('http://127.0.0.1:8000' + url, init). Node's fetch (undici) works in Node 24. But the response objects returned by undici are fine for the app's usage (response.ok, .status, .text()). ✓\n\nlocalStorage: jsdom supports localStorage when the URL is http(s) ✓ (I set url: \"http://localhost:5173/\").\n\nAlso the app's App.tsx imports \"./index.css\" → in lib mode, CSS is emitted separately; the import is dropped. ✓ But wait — Tailwind CSS import in a lib build → the tailwindcss vite plugin will process it. Fine.\n\nLet me write the test script.\n\nOne concern: the lib build of test-entry includes React and ReactDOM → ~250KB, fine.\n\nAnother concern: React 19's `createRoot(el).render()` in production mode — fine.\n\nLet me write the files.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794349016404,"end":1794349016415}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794349016441,"sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_01a1c78922447332b2dc01ce","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"import { createRoot } from \"react-dom/client\";\nimport App from \"../src/App\";\n\nexport function mount(container: HTMLElement) {\n  createRoot(container).render(<App />);\n}\n","filePath":"/workspace/frontend/scripts/test-entry.tsx"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/workspace/frontend/scripts/test-entry.tsx","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"frontend/scripts/test-entry.tsx","time":{"start":1794349016412,"end":1794349016421}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c7892557001y4oKSKOOMotfEv","sessionID":"ses_e3881931cffeUV0aIWtfc95zIA","messageID":"msg_1c788c49c0017MjxFG8yYCWA3u"}}
